Perelman's L-length: L(γ) = ∫₀^τ √σ (R(γ(σ)) + |γ'(σ)|²) dσ. In flat space R=0. The minimizer of ∫ √σ |γ'|² dσ with γ(0)=0, γ(τ)=q is found by Euler-Lagrange: d/dσ (2√σ γ') = 0, so √σ γ' = const, γ' = c/√σ, γ(σ) = 2c√σ. With γ(τ)=q: c = q/(2√τ), so γ(σ) = (√σ/√τ) q. This traces the straight ray through q (the geometric image is the ray {t q}), parameterized by √σ. So "the L-geodesics from the origin are straight rays" means: the *image* of the L-geodesic is the Euclidean segment/ray from 0 to q — the curve lies on the straight line through 0 and q. In the D7 model, the parametrization by σ is the reparametrization of the straight segment. So a "straight ray" statement could be phrased as: the L-geodesic is a scalar multiple of q at every time, i.e., ∀σ, γ(σ) = (something) • q — which D7's gaussianPath already encodes. Or more generally: any L-minimizer must be of this form (uniqueness of minimizers). The D7 proof shows the minimum value; for the *uniqueness/rigidity* one can show that if P is a minimizer then equality in completing-the-square forces γ'(σ) = (1/√σ) c a.e., hence γ(σ) = 2c√σ.

Actually, the task says: "Prove unconditionally: in flat ℝⁿ the L-geodesics from the origin are straight rays". So D11 should prove that every L-minimizer from the origin is (up to a.e. equality / continuous identification) the straight ray: γ(σ) = (√σ/√τ)•q, i.e. its image lies in the line spanned by q. That's the uniqueness/rigidity statement, a strengthening of D7's minimality (which only proved the minimal *value*).

